# ── What the review established ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ POD-LEVEL `spec.resources` IS KEPT BY THE API SERVER THE CLUSTER LANE RUNS. PodLevelResources is
# beta and on by default since 1.34; applied to rancher/k3s:v1.35.7-k3s1 the pod came back with
# `spec.resources.limits` and `requests` as rendered, no container-level resources, and
# `status.qosClass: Guaranteed` (2026-09-23, CyberCloud.Providers.ContainerInstance.Cluster.Conformance).
#
# ⚠ A POD'S LOG IS THE KUBELET'S AND IS READ THROUGH THE `log` SUBRESOURCE. A container that has not
# started answers HTTP 400 "container … is waiting to start: ContainerCreating", which KubeApiClient
# reads as OperationInProgress; a container name the pod does not have is a 400 of a different
# sentence and is the caller's mistake. PreconditionAndLogTests reads both against k3s.
#
# ⚠ A DELETED POD WITH RUNNING CONTAINERS STAYS READABLE FOR ITS GRACE PERIOD, carrying
# `deletionTimestamp` and `deletionGracePeriodSeconds`, and a process that is PID 1 ignores SIGTERM
# unless it handles it — measured as a full ten-second drain for `exec sleep`. The replace path waits
# for the pod to be gone because the new pod takes its name.
#
# ⚠ Kube-OVN names the IP object it allocates for a pod on the default provider `{pod}.{namespace}`
# (pkg/util, PodNameToPortName), which is what an OvnFip's `ipName` names; an OvnFip's `ipType` is
# left out, because the controller treats anything but `vip` as an IP object and the definition's
# own description of the field ("ipv4, ipv6, dual") describes a different thing. Read at v1.16.2;
# no lane runs Kube-OVN, so the translation itself is unmeasured — conformance.yaml § owed,
# `the-public-address-is-rendered-and-not-routed`.
upstream-webhook: none; a pod is admitted by the API server's own validation and the namespace's Pod Security level, and an OvnFip by its structural schema alone
